// Tests for section heading trailing-macro line-number shift (BUG D).
//
// BUG D — section heading ending with inline macro drops trailing newlines (Pass 2):
//   When a \section* heading ends with a custom macro (e.g. \MACRO1) rather than plain
//   text, the \n at end of the heading line and the blank \n that follows both fall
//   into the extras gap between consecutive AST markup nodes. qdocCollectProse never
//   writes them to the prose builder, so all subsequent alerts are shifted N lines
//   earlier (one per dropped \n). Each such heading adds 2 to the cumulative offset.
//
//   Trigger condition: BOTH of the following must hold:
//     1. \section1 heading ENDS with a macro — no trailing plain text.
//        '\section1 Title with \MACRO1 IDE' does NOT trigger (IDE absorbs the gap).
//     2. The first non-blank token after the blank line is \l{target}{\MACRO}
//        where the ALIAS is a macro command. A plain alias \l{target}{text}
//        does NOT trigger.
//   Indentation of the block comment does NOT affect whether the bug fires.
//
//   Fix (doc-level, no parser change required): either
//     a) Append plain text after the macro in the heading:
//        '\section1 Deploying with \QC IDE'  (IDE absorbs the \n\n gap)
//     b) Use a plain-text alias in the \l command:
//        '\l{\QC Documentation}{Qt Creator}'

// BUG D — plain alias contrast: \l aliases are plain text, so BUG D does NOT fire.
/*!
    \class QRuntimeLoader
    \brief Loads resources at run-time.

    \section1 Startup behavior on \MACRO1

    \l{QObject}{QObjects} are initialized when the application starts.
    All components are loaded from the resource bundle automatically.

    \section1 Shutdown with \BUILDVAR

    \l{QObject::deleteLater()}{deleteLater()} is called before the loop stops.
    All resources are released when the object is destroyed.
*/
// Expected (plain aliases — correct lines, BUG D not triggered):
//   34: Microsoft.Passive 'are initialized'
//   35: Microsoft.Passive 'are loaded'
//   39: Microsoft.Passive 'is called'
//   40: Microsoft.Passive 'are released', 'is destroyed'

// BUG D — macro alias trigger: \section1 ends with \QC, next paragraph opens with
//   \l{...}{\QC} (alias is a macro). Mirrors the deployment.qdoc false positive.
/*!
\page qtest-bugd.html
\title Deploying Test Applications
\brief Provides information on how to run test applications.

Test documents are loaded and run by the test runtime. This includes the
main engine along with the built-in module types and plugins. The
runtime also provides access to third-party modules.

Applications that use this system must invoke the runtime to run documents. You
can do this by creating a QTestView or a QTestEngine, as described below. In
addition, the package includes the \c qtest tool, which loads
\c {.qml} files. This tool is useful for developing and testing code without
having to write a C++ application to load the runtime.

\section1 Deploying applications with \QC

\l{\QC Documentation}{\QC} deploys and packages applications to various
platforms. The resources are loaded from the bundle automatically.
All components are released when the object is destroyed.
*/
// Expected (after fix):
//   68: Microsoft.Passive 'are loaded'              (BUG D: currently reported at 66)
//   69: Microsoft.Passive 'are released', 'is destroyed'  (BUG D: currently reported at 67)
